{"id":"int-cfaec041","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-09-18T15:46:05.1356166Z","actor":"Adam <la-xirsoi> Chevalier","issue_id":"hive-scn","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"in_progress","reason":"Hoisted the header set into containers/frontend/security-headers.conf, included by the server block and by every location that declares an add_header of its own; /healthz uses default_type instead. Verified on the running stack: /, /index.html, a deep link, a fingerprinted asset, /favicon.ico and /healthz all return all six headers. /idp keeps its own set and now sends HSTS once (proxy_hide_header drops Keycloak's shorter one)."}}
{"id":"int-b14205bf","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-09-18T16:39:01.9512597Z","actor":"Adam <la-xirsoi> Chevalier","issue_id":"hive-m50","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"in_progress","reason":"Removed offline_access from the requested scope in both environment files. Keycloak issues a refresh token from the plain authorization-code flow anyway (verified: refresh_expires_in 1800), so silent refresh is unaffected, and an offline token is pointless against a token set held in sessionStorage. Verified end to end against the running stack: authorize -> login as ada -> code exchange -> refresh_token present -> GET /api/v1/users/me returns 200. Not browser-verified; the Chrome extension was not connected."}}
